WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.410
They are edgy, rebellious and popular.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.190
IC3PEAK is on stage in Ryazan for the final
concert of their Russia tour.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:15.369
The song "Sad bitch" is clearly an audience
favorite here

00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:19.480
It's an anthem against authority, elitism
and conformism.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.160
Songs like this one have made the authorities
in Russia suspicious of IC3PEAK.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.519
The band say they shut down more than half
of their concerts on their recent Russia tour.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:32.310
The musicians were searched and questioned
several times.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.199
“It felt completely surreal.

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:41.960
We sing a few songs and the secret services
chase us through the whole city.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.370
Yes, some of our songs do have a political
message.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.910
But the political stuff is such a small percentage
of our work.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:55.890
So you think to yourself: What would happen
if we were actually a political group?

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:01.010
They wouldn't let us perform at all."

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:06.590
"I pour kerosene into my eyes.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.490
The whole of Russia is watching me.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.190
Let it all burn."

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.950
Lyrics and videos like this one are intentionally
provocative.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.400
Here the duo poses in front of the seat of
the Russian government – and on the shoulders

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.800
of the riot police.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.230
IC3PEAK isn't the only young group that has
had problems with the authorities.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.050
Last year, over forty concerts were cancelled.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:33.080
It's a crackdown that has shaken the Russian
music scene – but also created a huge sense

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.680
of solidarity.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.000
At the end of 2018 dozens of musicians took
to the stage in Moscow to protest against

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.520
the tough stance of the Kremlin.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:50.310
"If these absurd things can happen to other
musicians, they can easily happen to any of

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.310
us."

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:56.130
"Lately there seems to be an unspoken rule
for musicians: If you so much as touch on

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.080
politics – you'll have problems."

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:04.910
"This situation would make any musician want
to write about it – it politicizes people

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:10.319
who otherwise wouldn't be interested in political
protest at all."

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:14.120
Vladimir Putin seems to have become aware
of that tool - and is pushing for a change

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.900
in strategy.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.890
The Russian president wants his administration
to come up with new plans to support youth

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:20.890
culture by September.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:31.400
"Rap and other modern music is based on three
pillars: sex, drugs, and protest.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.859
Our biggest concern is the drugs of course.

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:40.989
They clearly lead to a degradation of the
entire society.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:49.379
If this music is impossible to stop, then
we have to lead and direct it properly."

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:54.659
However Putin tries to "lead and direct" - observers
say the Kremlin will do all in its power to

00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:58.569
ensure the discontent doesn't turn into a
protest movement.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:04.010
"What we've seen so far is an attempt by the
Kremlin to label anything they don't understand

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.870
as nonexistent, useless and wrong.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:10.769
But now the main thing for them is to reestablish
communication with young people – to understand

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:14.540
what they want and how to deal with it."

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:24.060
In Ryasan, the police presence has led some
parents to accompany their kids to the concert.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.500
Most of the fans here say they identify with
IC3PEAK's lyrics: they're often about a sense

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.079
of hopelessness.

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:37.069
“I can really relate to this music – I
think it reflects my reality here.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:38.680
I know exactly what they mean."

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.450
"The fact that the government is cracking
down on this group doesn't solve the problems

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:50.389
they are singing about – instead it could
lead to protests."

00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:54.629
The band can clearly relate to the worries
and realities of their young fans.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.599
Many of them say they don't feel the Kremlin
understands them anymore at all.

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:05.720
"The government has decided to “correct”
youth culture, to somehow bend it back into

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.419
its old shape.

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:10.489
But the process can't be stopped anymore.

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:12.489
And we certainly won't censor ourselves."

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:17.280
"Now we want to be even harsher, more radical
and interesting."

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:28.500
"You can't ban music!" the fans chant in between
songs at the concert.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.780
IS3PEAKs dark and sarcastic music has clearly
touched a nerve with young Russians.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.690
And their difficulties with the authorities
have attracted a lot of publicity.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:43.290
Now IC3PEAK has become one of the voices of
a new, critical generation who are no longer

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.970
to remain silent about Russia's political
realities.

